coder › Implemented `toRoman`/`fromRoman` in `src/roman.ts`, added tests in `src/roman.test.ts`.

What changed:
- `src/roman.ts`: `toRoman` uses a greedy value table (M, CM, D, CD, C, XC, L, XL, X, IX, V, IV, I); guards with `Number.isInteger(n) || n<1 || n>3999` and throws `RangeError`. `fromRoman` uppercases input, validates against standard-numeral regex `^M{0,3}(CM|CD|D?C{0,3})(XC|XL|L?X{0,3})(IX|IV|V?I{0,3})$`, throws `SyntaxError` with the text (`JSON.stringify(text)`) embedded on failure (covers `""`, `IIII`, `VX`, `IC`, etc.), then sums left-to-right with subtract-when-smaller-than-next.
- `src/roman.test.ts`: single symbols, all six subtractive forms, known values (3, 14, 44, 99, 944, 1984, 2026, 3999), `RangeError` cases (0, -1, 4000, 10000, 1.5, NaN, Infinity), `SyntaxError` cases with message-naming check, lowercase acceptance, and full 1–3999 round-trip `fromRoman(toRoman(n)) === n`.

Decisions not in the request: chose `RangeError` message `out of range, expected an integer from 1 to 3999: ${n}`; chose `SyntaxError` message `invalid Roman numeral: ${JSON.stringify(text)}` so the offending text is always named (including empty string); accepted mixed case via `toUpperCase()`; validation via the canonical regex.

